Herein, the microRNA-141 electrochemiluminescence (ECL) bioassay was developed using the dual-ligand metalorganic framework (d-MOF) with ordered heterogeneity, which simultaneously contained the luminophore li­ gands (1,1,2,2-tetra(4-carboxylbiphenyl)ethylene, denoted as TCBPE) and the coreactant ligands (1,4-dia­ zabicyclo[2.2.2]octane, denoted as $\mathrm{DN}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2})$ . The resultant d-MOF revealed significantly enhanced ECL intensity without any exogenous coreactants, which was 3.53 times higher in comparison with that of single-ligand MOF (only TCBPE as ligands) even with the addition of exogenous $\mathrm{DN}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ . Thanks to the ordered heterogeneity in dMOF, the intramolecular rotation of TCBPE was restricted via oriented coordination and the spatial location of $\mathrm{DN}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ was reasonably arranged due to the framework structure, which could not only enhance the excitation efficiency but also improve the electron-transfer efficiency based on the synergistic enhancement effect between structures and compositions in micro/nano confined space. Based on this, the proposed biosensor employed a novel DNA triangular prism (DNA TP) as signal switch to detect microRNA-141, achieving the low detection limit at the level of $22.9\ \mathrm{aM}$ and a broad linear ranging from $100\ \mathrm{aM}$ to $100\ \mathrm{pM}$ . The precise design of the ordered dMOFs by co-assembling the luminophore and coreactant ligands holds a promise strategy to achieve ECL MOFs and construct the ECL biosensors in diagnostic analysis.
## 1. Introduction
Polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons (PAHs) with intense luminescence and adjustable emissions have widespread applications in electro­ chemiluminescence (ECL) biosensing for the determination of proteins, enzymes, nucleic acids, and so on (Gao et al., 2019; Zhang et al., 2021). Recent studies have shown that PAH nanocrystals displayed enhanced ECL emission in the aqueous phase because the aggregation state of PAHs in nanocrystals could restrict intermolecular rotation (Liu et al., 2021; Wurthner, 2020). Nevertheless, the inner filter effect and the low excitation efficiency originating from aggregation state might become a bottleneck for luminous efficiency improvement. As a type of widely reported porous materials, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) with extended networks by linking metal nodes and organic ligands displayed the promising structures, inherent porosity and ultrahigh surface area (Huang et al., 2022; Yu et al., 2020). In particular, the unique framework could provide the orderly arrangement of luminophore ligands with appropriate spacing, being beneficial to excite the internal lumino­ phores. More recently, various ECL MOFs have been developed with PAHs as organic ligands, revealing enhanced luminescence efficiency compared with that of PAH nanocrystals. Xiao and his colleagues pre­ pared an ECL MOF by linking 1,1,2,2-tetra(4-carboxylbiphenyl)ethylene (TCBPE) and Hf nodes, which led to a strong ECL emission in aqueous solution containing coreactant triethylamine (TEA) (Huang et al., 2020). Subsequently, Yao’s group synthesized the PCN-222 MOFs by using meso-tetra(carboxyphenyl)porphyrin (TCPP) as the luminophore li­ gands, generating strong and stable ECL intensity (Li et al., 2022). The mentioned works proved that the network structures of MOFs allowed the orderly arrangement of PAHs to promote the excitation efficiency in chemiluminescence process. However, the ECL efficiency is also influenced by the reaction efficiency of free radicals in electrochemical process, as well as excitation efficiency in chemiluminescence process. How to improve the reaction efficiency of the two processes to obtain better ECL performance?
Recently, it is reported the nanomaterials with encapsulation or postmodification of coreactants could enhance the intermolecular reaction efficiency in the electrochemical reaction process owing to the syner­ gistic effects between structures and components (Song et al., 2022). In our previous works, the high intramolecular reaction efficiency was achieved by employing the self-enhanced ECL molecules composed of luminophores and coreactants (Zhao et al., 2015; Zhuo et al., 2014), which could shorten the mass transfer distance, reduce energy loss and improve electron transfer efficiency. Thus, it is reasonable to assume that the significantly enhanced ECL would be achieved at the dual-ligand MOFs (d-MOFs) with the co-coordination of luminophores and coreactants, which is favorable to accelerate the reaction efficiency of both chemiluminescence and electrochemical processes.
MicroRNAs (miRNAs) play a crucial regulatory role throughout many biological processes, whose abnormal expression is related to the occurrence and development of various cancers (Huntzinger and Izaurralde, 2011; Treiber et al., 2019). One of the biggest challenges in miRNAs discrimination is to design efficient amplification strategies because of their high sequence homology and low abundance. Despite nucleic acid signal amplification strategies of strand displacement amplification (SDA) (Hu et al., 2022) and polymerase chain reaction (PCR) (Qi et al., 2018; Wang et al., 2022) can amplify trace target nucleic acid molecules thousands of times, it still has shortcomings of multistep reactions, time-consuming operation and high equipment re­ quirements. As an alternative approach, the DNA nanodevices have become a hotspot for target amplification due to their excellent controllable structure, easy modification and programmable sequence (Jung et al., 2016; Zhao et al., 2021). For example, Zhang’s group designed a three-dimensional DNA machine to output the secondary target, achieving signal amplification for electrochemical biosensing (Zhao et al., 2019). Moreover, Zhu and colleagues constructed an ECL biosensor with DNA triggered bipedal DNA nanoswitch to introduce mimic targets onto the electrode surface by magnetic separation for signal output (Wang et al., 2021). Nevertheless, these DNA nanodevice-based biosensors were still involved in complicated steps of target amplification or transformation and signal switch activation. Thus, it is important to develop DNA nanodevices which could simplify the target recognition and direct signal output into one step, achieving quick and efficient sensing process.
In this work, a DNA triangular prism (DNA TP) was designed inge­ niously and anchored on ECL d-MOF modified electrode as the signal switch to realize target recognition and signal output in one step for the sensitive detection of miRNA-141. Specifically, the d-MOF was synthe­ sized by solvothermal method via co-coordination of the luminophore ligands TCBPE and the coreactant ligands 1,4-diazabicyclo [2.2.2] oc­ tane $\mathrm{(DN_{2}H_{2})}$ onto the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ nodes, whose ECL intensity considerably enhanced in an aqueous media (Scheme 1A). According to the optimi­ zation of appropriate coreactant ligands, we found the proposed d-MOF exhibited highly ordered heterogeneity and desirable ECL intensity without any exogenous coreactants. Furthermore, the d-MOF and gold nanoparticles (Au NPs) were coated onto the pretreated electrode sur­ face to anchor the DNA TP, which acquired the “signal off” state as the quenching effect of Fc molecular (Scheme 1B). When miRNA-141 was present, the protected-swing arms on DNA TP were directly activated and subsequently hybridized with Fc-DNA to make Fc release with the cleavage of T7 Exo, achieving the signal recovery. Impressively, d-MOF displayed the intense and stable ECL which opened a new insight for achieving luminous MOFs and constructing the ECL biosensors in diagnostic analysis.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Materials and reagents
The Experimental Section of the Supporting Information (SI) con­ tained all of the materials and reagents utilized in this work.
## 2.2. Synthesis of dual-ligand MOF
The synthesis conditions of d-MOF were referred to the report before (Zhu et al., 2021). The Zn $(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $3.6~\mathrm{mg})$ , 1,1,2,2-tetra (4-car­ boxylbiphenyl) ethylene (TCBPE, $9.7~\mathrm{\mg}$ ), 1,4-diazabicyclo [2.2.2] noctane $\mathrm{(DN_{2}H_{2}}$ , $0.67\mathrm{mg}$ ) and three drops of formic acid were mixed in N, N-dimethylformamide (DMF, $2.5\mathrm{mL}$ ). The above mixed solution was stirred for $30\mathrm{min}$ at room temperature and then put into a Teflon-lined autoclave and heated at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 2 days. After the hydrothermal re­ action, the precipitate was washed with DMF several times, centrifuged, and dispersed in $3~\mathrm{mL}$ of deionized water to obtain the d-MOF.
## 2.3. Synthesis of single-ligand MOF
The synthesis method of single-ligand MOF (s-MOF) was like that of d-MOF in the absence of $\mathrm{DH}_{2}\mathrm{N}_{2}$ . Specifically, the Zn $(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (3.6 mg) and TCBPE $(9.7~\mathrm{mg})$ were mixed in DMF $\mathrm{\Omega}_{2.5\mathrm{\mL}}$ and heated at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 2 days in a Teflon-lined autoclave. Then, the precipitate could be obtained via washing with DMF several times, centrifuging, and dispersing in $3\mathrm{mL}$ of deionized water.
## 2.4. Preparation of the DNA TP nanoswitch
As shown in Scheme 1B, first, the equal molars $(8\upmu\mathrm{M})$ of S1, S2, S3, and S4 were dissolved in the annealing buffer (Tris-Magnesium sulfate buffer, TM buffer), respectively, and then mixed to obtain the welldesigned DNA scaffolds by using programmable annealing, which were further treated to reduce their disulfide bonds (see in SI, section S1.1). Then, $20\upmu\mathrm{L}$ protected strands $(4\upmu\mathrm{M})$ and $20\upmu\mathrm{L}$ swing arm strands $(4\upmu\up M)$ were mixed and kept in $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ oven for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ to generate the hybrid protected-swing arms. Eventually, the above solution was incubated with the prepared DNA scaffold at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ to generate DNA TP nanoswitch $(1~\upmu\mathrm{M})$ .
## 2.5. Preparation of the proposed ECL biosensor
Before using, we polished the bare glassy carbon electrode (GCE) by $\mathrm{\Al_{2}O_{3}}$ powder and washed it with ethanol and water according to the previous literature (Tao et al., 2022). Afterwards, $5\upmu\mathrm{L}$ d-MOF solution was decorated on the resultant electrode surface and further dried at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to form the d-MOF modified electrode. Next, $5~{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ the Au NPs solution (Section S3 of SI, $16\mathrm{{nm}}\cdot$ ) was coated on the surface of resultant electrode and then dried in an oven at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to obtain the Au NPs modified d-MOF/GCE. Then, DNA TP solution $(10~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ was decorated onto the modified electrode for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ via Au–S bonds, which was gently rinsed with the $\mathrm{pH}~7.4$ phosphate buffered saline once (PBS, Section S1.1 of SI). After the $10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ hexanethiol (HT, $1.0\ \mathrm{mM})$ was incubated onto the DNA TP/Au NPs/d-MOF/GCE for $40\mathrm{min}$ to restrain the nonspecific bindings, the obtained biosensor was rinsed with PBS again.
## 2.6. ECL measurement procedure
Briefly, the $100~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ reaction solution containing miRNA-141 $(10~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ with a series of concentrations, $1000\mathrm{U/mL}$ T7 Exo $(10~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ , and $1\times\mathrm{NE}$ buffer 4 $(80\upmu\mathrm{L})$ was incubated with the proposed biosensor at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and kept $^\mathrm{~3~h~}$ . Next, in order to remove the unreacted residue on the biosensor, the above electrode was rinsed with PBS. By detecting the constructed biosensor in $2~\mathrm{mL}$ PBS with potential from 0 to $1.4\mathrm{V}$ with the photomultiplier tube (PMT) setting at $700\mathrm{V}$ , the ECL responses were recorded.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characterizations of the prepared d-MOF
Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and transmission electron mi­ croscopy (TEM) were employed to characterize the morphology and size of the prepared d-MOF. As shown in Fig. 1A, the prepared d-MOF exhibited stick-like structures with micro-level dimensions. In Fig. 1B, there were several different lattices on the surface of the d-MOF covering 0.590, 0.596, and $0.605~\mathrm{nm}$ . As shown in the powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) pattern in Fig. 1C, there were some peaks in the $2\theta$ ranging from $5^{\circ}$ to $30^{\circ}$ . These results demonstrated that d-MOF with integration of dual ligands exhibited regular morphology and perfect crystalline co­ ordination networks. In addition, fluorescence (FL) microscopy image of the d-MOF was showed with bright green glow upon the excitation wavelength of ${375}\mathrm{nm}$ in Fig. 1D. The morphology and size of the d-MOF in bright and dark field (Fig. 1D and E) were well agreed with those of the SEM image in Fig. 1A. The thermogravimetric (TG) curve was dis­ played in Fig. 1F to investigate the stability of d-MOF. It began to collapse only at around $389^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with the loss of ligands, which revealed that the framework of the d-MOF had good thermostability for appli­ cation in biosensors.
## 3.2. Optical and electrical performance of the d-MOF
To analyze optical characteristics of the synthetic d-MOF and TCBPE monomers in solution, the UV–vis and FL spectra were measured. All samples were dissolved in deionized water with equal molar amount of luminophores. In Fig. 2A, the UV–vis spectrum of d-MOF exhibited a sharp characteristic absorption peak $(271\mathrm{nm}$ , curve b), which was blueshifted compared with that of TCBPE solution $(290~\mathrm{nm}$ , curve a). Moreover, the absorption of d-MOF was 2.95 times higher than that of TCBPE under the same condition. Similarly, as displayed in Fig. 2B, compared to FL emission of the TCBPE (curve $\grave{a_{\phi}}$ ), that of d-MOF had stronger and blue-shifted peak under the excitation of the same wave­ length $360.0\ \mathrm{nm}$ , curve b). Specifically, the d-MOF showed a blue emission with a higher emission peak at $447.6\ \mathrm{nm}$ , while the TCBPE exhibited a yellow-green emission peak at $480.4~\mathrm{nm}$ (inset in Fig. 2B). This enhancement and blue-shift phenomenon might be assigned to the well-defined framework matrix, which could regulate the orderly arrangement of ligands and limit their vibrational rotation (Takezawa et al., 2020; Yin et al., 2019). Subsequently, the ECL response curves and the three-dimensional (3D) ECL spectrum were obtained by detecting the TCBPE modified GCE (TCBPE/GCE) and d-MOF modified GCE (d-MOF/GCE) in $2\mathrm{mL}$ PBS when the potential was ranged from 0 to 1.4 V. Furthermore, the 3D ECL spectrum of d-MOF/GCE (in Fig. 2C) dis­ played the maximum wavelength at $522.5~\mathrm{nm}$ , which exhibited a 74.9 nm red shift in comparison with its FL emission. This appearance could be explained by the formation of free radicals under the excitation process of electrochemistry leading to a different micro-environment for the excited state (Kim et al., 2016). Moreover, in Fig. 2D, the ECL in­ tensity and stability of d-MOF/GCE in PBS without exogenous cor­ eactants (curve $b$ ) had significantly enhanced by comparison with that of TCBPE/GCE in PBS containing 2 mM $\mathrm{DN}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ (curve a). This could be attributed to the synergistic effects of vibration-limited enhancement
ECL (Cao et al., 2022; Liao et al., 2021), which were the benefits from the well-defined framework matrix and the highly ordered assembly of TCBPE ligands.
In addition, the possible ECL mechanism of d-MOF was further investigated via differential pulse voltammetry (DPV) and cyclic vol­ tammetry (CV) (see in SI, section S7). As shown in Fig. S4A, the two oxidation peaks of $\mathrm{DN}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ were observed. This phenomenon could indicate the secondary oxidation process of $\mathrm{DN}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , which was consis­ tent with that reported in the literature (Zhu et al., 2021). Ultimately, the possible ECL mechanism was described as following reaction equations.
$$
{\bf d-M O F-e}={\bf d-M O F}^{+\bullet}
$$
$$
\mathrm{DN}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\cdot\mathbf{e}=\mathrm{DN}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}^{+\bullet}
$$
$$
\mathrm{DN}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}^{+\bullet}-\mathrm{H}^{+}=\mathrm{DN}_{2}\mathrm{H}^{\bullet}
$$
$$
\mathrm{DN_{2}H^{\bullet}+d\mathbf{-}M O F^{+\bullet}=d\mathbf{-}M O F^{\ast}}
$$
