## 3.3. ECL performance comparison under different conditions
Four types of TCBPE under different states were prepared and the corresponding ECL response curves were obtained as (a) the TCBPE in the monodisperse state, (b) TCBPE microcrystals $_{.\pi-\pi}$ stacked state, synthesis method in SI), (c) s-MOF (coordination TCBPE in the frame­ work), and (d) d-MOF (co-coordination TCBPE and $\mathrm{DH}_{2}\mathrm{N}_{2}$ in the framework). As presented in Fig. 3A, the increased signal of TCBPE microcrystals modified electrode (curve $b$ ) compared to that of the TCBPE in the monodisperse state (curve $a$ ) stemmed from the reduction of vibrational and rotation in the stacked state. Then, the ECL signal was further enhanced when the s-MOF was modified onto the electrode surface (curve c) because of the orderly arrangement of TCBPE and high porosity in the framework. Amazingly, the ECL signal of d-MOF (17193 a.u, curve $d)$ was significantly increased by 3.53 times over that of sMOF even without the additional $\mathrm{DN}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ . Moreover, the ECL efficiency of the d-MOF was increased by 3.36 times than that of the s-MOF ac­ cording to ECL efficiency calculation equation (calculation process in SI, section S8) (Wang et al., 2016). The obviously improved ECL intensity strength and efficiency of $\mathbf{d}$ -MOF could be attributed to two reasons as shown in Fig. 3B. First, the framework would restrict the TCBPE vibra­ tion and provide a highly ordered arrangement of non-planar lumines­ cent molecules, which regulated intermolecular distances and further increased the chemiluminescence efficiency. Second, the framework with the co-coordination of TCBPE and $\mathrm{DH}_{2}\mathrm{N}_{2}$ would enhance the electrochemical efficiency because the confined framework could facilitate the survival of free radicals, reduce energy loss, and shorten the mass and electron transfer distance.
Subsequently, to optimize the effect of the alternative coreactant ligands on ECL performance, other three tertiary amine ligands, N, Ndiethylethylenediamine (DEED, curve $a\mathrm{i}$ , triethylamine (TEA, curve b), and tripropylamine (TPrA, curve $\overrightarrow{c}.$ ), were covalently linked into MOF matrix by replacing $\mathrm{DN}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , respectively. As shown in Fig. 3C, the ECL strength enhanced successively from curve $a$ to d. As expected, the dMOF had the most prominent ECL intensity (curve $d)$ since the $\mathrm{DN}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ composed of two tertiary amines was more conducive to formation of coordination bonds with metal nodes and production of free radicals under electrochemical reaction process (Zhao et al., 2022). In addition, when these tertiary amine reagents were used as exogenous coreactants (Fig. 3D), the strongest ECL intensity was achieved with $\mathrm{DN}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ (curve d), indicating it also was the excellent coreactant in the intermolecular reaction. In conclusion, the d-MOF built by co-coordination of $\mathrm{DN}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and TCBPE demonstrated the greatest ECL intensity due to the increased efficiency of both the electrochemical and the chemiluminescence re­ action process.
## 3.4. Feasibility analysis of the constructed biosensor
The construction of the DNA scaffold was confirmed by poly­ acrylamide gel electrophoresis (PAGE). As shown in Fig. 4A, the distinct bands of lanes 1–4 presented the S1–S4, respectively, which possessed different migration rates owing to the different numbers of their bases. When the mixture of the four DNA strands was added in lane 5, a bright and single band emerged, confirming the successful construction of DNA scaffold with one-pot self-assembly. With the advantages in character­ izing the morphology of materials at the nanoscale, atomic force mi­ croscopy (AFM) could provide valuable information for the identification and resolution of DNA nanostructures (Gross et al., 2018; Hosono et al., 2019). Therefore, AFM imaging was initially used to verify the assembly of DNA scaffold. Fig. 4B showed a homogeneous height about $3\mathrm{nm}$ which was close to the theoretical value according to the calculation rules of the reported literature (Peng et al., 2018), con­ firming the successful construction of DNA scaffold by the one-pot self-assembly process.
Then, in Fig. 4C, distinct single bands could be clearly observed at different positions of the strip after loading the protect stand, S1, swing arm, miRNA-141 and protected-swing arm in lanes 1–5, respectively. The addition of miRNA-141 and the protected swing arm produced two bright bands in lane 6, of which the bottom band possessed a lower migration than the protected strand (lane 1) and miRNA-141 (lane 4), presuming it was the hybridization of the two. Subsequently, the above hybridization band occurred again in lane 7 when the miRNA-141 existed compared with lane 8 (mixture of the protected-swing arm and S1). These results demonstrated that the “on” state of the nanoswitch could be observed only in the presence of miRNA-141.
The ECL analysis technique was implemented to prove the feasibility analysis of the constructed biosensor. The negligible ECL intensity of bare GCE was observed (curve a) in PBS. Then, the d-MOF was modified onto the GCE surface, achieving an obvious ECL intensity (curve $b$ ) as seen in Fig. 4A. When Au NPs were decorated on the resultant electrode, negligible degradation of ECL signal was generated (curve c). Subse­ quently, after depositing the DNA TP onto the electrode interface via the Au–S bonds, the ECL signal was evidently decreased because of the quenching effect of Fc molecules (curve d). Next, HT was incubated on the modified electrode to block the unreacted sites with the insignificant ECL signal decrease (curve e). Finally, the reaction solution with the 100 pM miRNA-141 and 100 U T7 Exo was further incubated on the abovemodified GCE. As expected, the ECL signal successfully recovered (curve $f)$ , which was caused by removing the Fc away from the electrode interface. In short, the above data proved the feasibility of the biosensor.
## 3.5. Performance of the as-proposed biosensor toward miRNA-141 detection
The well-designed biosensor was used to detect the miRNA-141 under the optimal conditions to identify the performance of the ECL biosensor. As displayed in Fig. 5A, when the concentrations of target increased from $100\ \mathrm{aM}$ to $100\ \mathrm{pM}$ , the ECL signals gradually increased along with them. Noticeably, the ECL signals and the logarithm value of miRNA-141 concentrations $(\lg c)$ had a favorable linear relationship. In addition, the regression equation was $I=1998.50\log c+35265.76$ and the detection limit was 22.9 aM $(\mathsf{S}/\mathsf{N}=3)$ with the linear correlation coefficient (R) of 0.9998. Compared with other previous works, the prepared biosensor had a superior analytical performance toward miRNA detection as shown in SI (Table S2). In addition, selectivity and stability of this biosensor were also studied. As seen in Fig. 5C, the constructed ECL biosensor had good stability after 18 cycles of contin­ uous potential scanning to obtain the relative standard deviation (RSD) at $0.432\%$ . As depicted in Fig. 5D, even if the concentration of other interferents was $100~{\upmu\mathrm{M}}$ , the ECL intensities of them still showed ignorable responses compared with background signal of the blank so­ lution. However, after mixing three interference miRNAs $(100~\upmu\mathrm{M})$ and target miRNA-141 $\mathrm{100~pM)}$ , the ECL responses displayed strong in­ tensities in accord with the result only in the presence of miRNA-141, indicating the suggested ECL biosensor with outstanding selectivity. All in all, these results showed that the prepared ECL biosensor had a great performance for miRNA-141 detection. Also, as-prepared biosensor was preliminarily applied in tumor cells were to investigate its feasibility in real samples (SI, Fig. S8).
## 4. Conclusions
Here, the d-MOF was meticulously designed by covalently inte­ grating the TCBPE luminophores and $\mathrm{DN}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ coreactants into ${\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ nodes with ordered heterogeneity. Owing to the synergistic effects be­ tween structures and compositions, the ECL intensity and efficiency of dMOF were significantly improved. First, the structures of MOF were beneficial for arranging the TCBPE luminophore in an orderly way with increased excitation efficiency in the chemiluminescence process. Sec­ ond, with the co-coordination of luminophores and coreactants into MOF matrix, the d-MOF demonstrated efficient electrochemical reaction efficiency because of the short electron transfer and less energy loss. Moreover, the constructed ECL biosensor realized a low detection limit and a wide linear range for miRNA-141 detection with a novel DNA TP nanoswitch, which directly combined the signal output and target identification for a speedy and efficient sensing process. However, one of the concerns is the dependence on nucleases could increase the assay cost and restrict the shelf life, resulting in the limitation of practical applications. In conclusion, this work provided a promising bioanalysis platform combining the ECL probe of d-MOF and the efficient nano­ switch of TP DNA for the development of ECL biosensor.
